videos.script:
Touch a list and drag it. The app has to answer your finger and draw the next picture, in time. {{finger}}

On a sixty hertz screen, in time means sixteen point six seven milliseconds for each frame. {{budget}} One thread does both jobs. It takes the touch, runs your code, and draws. It is called the main thread. {{thread}}

When your code holds that thread for longer than a frame, the screen misses its slot and the list stutters. That stutter has a name: jank. {{jank}} Hold the thread for seconds, and the system offers the user a way to close your app. {{anr}}

In this lesson you work out the budget, name what blocks the thread, and run an experiment. A program sorts three hundred thousand study sessions on the same thread as a frame clock, and counts the frames it drops. Then it sorts them on a worker beside it. {{worker}} Last, you see how Kotlin, Swift and React Native each move heavy work off the main thread, and bring the answer back. {{three}}
